--- DESCRIPTION ---
Retail data maths: percentages, averages, profit, mark-up and footfall, each with a formula and example.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Why Maths Matters in Retail</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Every retail business handles numbers daily — from working out how much profit a product makes to tracking how many customers walk through the door. Being able to calculate and interpret these figures is an essential skill for anyone working in retail. This lesson covers the core mathematical techniques you need: percentages, averages, profit, mark-up and footfall. For each one you will see the formula and a straightforward worked example using realistic retail figures.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Percentages and Percentage Change</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">A <dfn class="term" data-def="A number expressed as a fraction of 100, used to compare values on a common scale.">percentage</dfn> expresses one number as a fraction of another out of 100. To find what percentage one value is of another, divide the part by the whole and multiply by 100.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n5"><strong>Formula: Percentage = (Part &divide; Whole) &times; 100</strong></p>
<p data-narration-id="n6"><em>Worked example:</em> A clothing store sells 40 jackets out of a total stock of 160 items. What percentage of stock are jackets? (40 &divide; 160) &times; 100 = 25%. Jackets make up 25% of total stock.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n7"><dfn class="term" data-def="A calculation showing how much a value has increased or decreased, expressed as a percentage of the original value.">Percentage change</dfn> shows how much a figure has risen or fallen compared with its original value.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n8"><strong>Formula: Percentage change = ((New value &minus; Original value) &divide; Original value) &times; 100</strong></p>
<p data-narration-id="n9"><em>Worked example:</em> A supermarket&rsquo;s weekly sales were &pound;8,000 last month and &pound;10,000 this month. Percentage change = ((10,000 &minus; 8,000) &divide; 8,000) &times; 100 = (2,000 &divide; 8,000) &times; 100 = 25%. Sales have increased by 25%. A negative result means a decrease.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n10">Fractions and Ratios</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n11">Fractions are used to express part of a whole (e.g. &frac12; of stock sold). <dfn class="term" data-def="A comparison of two or more quantities showing how they relate to each other, written using a colon.">Ratios</dfn> compare two or more quantities directly and are often used to describe the split between values such as online versus in-store sales.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n12"><em>Worked example:</em> A retailer makes 300 sales in a week — 200 in-store and 100 online. The ratio of in-store to online sales is 200:100. Simplify by dividing both numbers by 100, giving a ratio of 2:1. This means two sales are made in-store for every one made online. Ratios are always simplified to their lowest whole numbers.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n13" data-revision-tip="Cover this and recite the percentage and percentage-change formulae from memory. Check you can correctly label the 'part', 'whole', 'new value' and 'original value' before applying the formula.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Percentage = (Part &divide; Whole) &times; 100. Percentage change = ((New &minus; Original) &divide; Original) &times; 100. A positive result is an increase; a negative result is a decrease. Always read the question carefully to identify which number is the &ldquo;original&rdquo; (the starting point).</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n14">Averages: Mean, Median and Mode</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n15">Retail businesses use averages to summarise large sets of data — for example, average daily sales or average transaction value. There are three types of average to know.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n16"><strong>Mean</strong> — add all the values together and divide by how many there are. This is the most commonly used average in retail data.<br/><em>Worked example:</em> A convenience store&rsquo;s daily sales for five days were: &pound;420, &pound;380, &pound;510, &pound;460, &pound;430. Mean = (420 + 380 + 510 + 460 + 430) &divide; 5 = 2,200 &divide; 5 = &pound;440.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n17"><strong>Median</strong> — arrange the values in order from smallest to largest; the median is the middle value. If there is an even number of values, find the mean of the two middle numbers.<br/><em>Worked example:</em> Using the same five figures in order: &pound;380, &pound;420, &pound;430, &pound;460, &pound;510. The middle value is the third number: &pound;430. The median daily sales figure is &pound;430.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n18"><strong>Mode</strong> — the value that appears most often. The mode is useful for identifying the most common price point or best-selling product.<br/><em>Worked example:</em> A sports retailer sells trainers in sizes 6, 7, 8, 8, 9, 8, 10. The mode (most frequent) is size 8. The retailer knows to stock more of that size.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n19" data-revision-tip="Cover this and name the three averages without looking. For mean: add then divide. For median: order the data, find the middle. For mode: most frequent. Each is useful in different retail contexts.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Mean = sum of all values &divide; number of values. Median = middle value when data are ordered (or mean of the two middle values for an even set). Mode = the most frequently occurring value. All three are valid &ldquo;averages&rdquo; — the exam may ask for a specific one, so read the question carefully.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n20">Profit</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n21"><dfn class="term" data-def="The financial gain made when revenue (income from sales) exceeds total costs; calculated as revenue minus costs.">Profit</dfn> is the money a business keeps after paying all its costs. It is one of the most important measures of business performance.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n22"><strong>Formula: Profit = Revenue &minus; Costs</strong></p>
<p data-narration-id="n23"><em>Worked example:</em> A gift shop earns &pound;15,000 in sales revenue in one month. Its total costs (rent, wages, stock, utilities) are &pound;11,500. Profit = &pound;15,000 &minus; &pound;11,500 = &pound;3,500. If costs were higher than revenue the result would be negative, meaning a loss.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>What is the difference between gross profit and net profit?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n24">Although the exam focuses on the basic profit formula, it is helpful to understand the difference. Gross profit is revenue minus the direct cost of the goods sold (the cost of buying in the stock). Net profit then subtracts all other operating costs — rent, wages, utilities and so on — from the gross profit. For the purposes of this qualification, &ldquo;profit&rdquo; typically refers to net profit: total revenue minus total costs. Always use the formula given in the question if one is provided.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n25">Percentage Profit Margin</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n26">The <dfn class="term" data-def="Profit expressed as a percentage of revenue, showing how much of every pound of sales is kept as profit.">percentage profit margin</dfn> shows how profitable a business is relative to its sales. It allows fair comparison between businesses of different sizes.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n27"><strong>Formula: Percentage profit margin = (Profit &divide; Revenue) &times; 100</strong></p>
<p data-narration-id="n28"><em>Worked example:</em> Using the gift shop above: profit = &pound;3,500 and revenue = &pound;15,000. Percentage profit margin = (3,500 &divide; 15,000) &times; 100 = 23.3% (rounded to one decimal place). This means the business keeps approximately 23p of every &pound;1 of sales as profit.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n29">Mark-Up</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n30">Mark-up is the amount added to the cost price of a product to set its selling price. It is expressed as a percentage of the cost price (unlike profit margin, which uses revenue as the base).</p>
<p data-narration-id="n31"><strong>Formula: Mark-up (%) = ((Selling price &minus; Cost price) &divide; Cost price) &times; 100</strong></p>
<p data-narration-id="n32"><em>Worked example:</em> A bookshop buys a novel for &pound;5 (cost price) and sells it for &pound;8 (selling price). Mark-up = ((8 &minus; 5) &divide; 5) &times; 100 = (3 &divide; 5) &times; 100 = 60%. The bookshop has marked up the product by 60% on cost. Retailers use mark-up to ensure they cover costs and generate a profit on every item sold.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>How is mark-up different from percentage profit margin?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n33">Both figures measure profitability on a single product, but they use a different base number in the calculation. Mark-up divides the profit by the cost price; percentage profit margin divides the profit by the selling price (revenue). Using the bookshop example: selling price &pound;8, cost price &pound;5, profit &pound;3. Mark-up = (3 &divide; 5) &times; 100 = 60%. Profit margin = (3 &divide; 8) &times; 100 = 37.5%. Mark-up will always give a higher percentage than profit margin for the same product. Knowing which formula to use depends on what the question asks.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n34">Sales and Footfall Calculations</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n35">Sales revenue is the total income a business receives from selling goods or services. <dfn class="term" data-def="The number of people who enter a retail store during a given period; a key indicator of a store's popularity and potential for sales.">Footfall</dfn> measures how many customers enter a store in a given period. Both are key performance indicators in retail.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n36"><strong>Sales revenue formula: Sales revenue = Price per unit &times; Number of units sold</strong><br/><em>Worked example:</em> A pharmacy sells 250 bottles of shampoo at &pound;4 each. Sales revenue = &pound;4 &times; 250 = &pound;1,000.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n37"><strong>Conversion rate formula: Conversion rate (%) = (Number of sales &divide; Footfall) &times; 100</strong><br/><em>Worked example:</em> On Saturday 800 customers entered a fashion store and 120 made a purchase. Conversion rate = (120 &divide; 800) &times; 100 = 15%. Only 15% of visitors converted to buyers, which might prompt the manager to investigate pricing or product range.</p>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n39">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n40">Percentage = (Part &divide; Whole) &times; 100; Percentage change = ((New &minus; Original) &divide; Original) &times; 100. A positive result is an increase; a negative result is a decrease.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n41">The three averages are mean (add then divide), median (middle value when ordered) and mode (most frequent value).</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n42">Profit = Revenue &minus; Costs. Percentage profit margin = (Profit &divide; Revenue) &times; 100. Mark-up = ((Selling price &minus; Cost price) &divide; Cost price) &times; 100.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n43">Sales revenue = Price &times; Units sold. Conversion rate = (Sales &divide; Footfall) &times; 100. Always show working in the exam to maximise method marks.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n38">In the exam, show every step of your working — you can still earn method marks even if your final answer is wrong. Always write the formula first, then substitute the numbers. Watch out for the difference between mark-up (divides by cost price) and profit margin (divides by revenue/selling price): using the wrong base is a very common mistake. For percentage change, check carefully whether the question asks for an increase or a decrease — a negative result means a fall in sales. When asked to interpret a figure (e.g. a 15% conversion rate), always link it to what it means for the business, not just what the number is.</p>

